<section>
  <h2>Eligible Countries and Travelers</h2>

  <p>
    The 240-hour visa-free transit policy currently applies to nationals of
    57 countries.
  </p>

  <h3>Eligible countries</h3>

  <h4>Europe â€” 40 countries</h4>
  <p>
    Austria, Belgium, Czech Republic, Denmark, Estonia, Finland, France,
    Germany, Greece, Hungary, Iceland, Italy, Latvia, Lithuania, Luxembourg,
    Malta, the Netherlands, Poland, Portugal, Slovakia, Slovenia, Spain,
    Sweden, Switzerland, Monaco, Russia, the United Kingdom, Ireland, Cyprus,
    Bulgaria, Romania, Ukraine, Serbia, Croatia, Bosnia and Herzegovina,
    Montenegro, North Macedonia, Albania, Belarus, and Norway.
  </p>

  <h4>Americas â€” 6 countries</h4>
  <p>
    United States, Canada, Brazil, Mexico, Argentina, and Chile.
  </p>

  <h4>Oceania â€” 2 countries</h4>
  <p>
    Australia and New Zealand.
  </p>

  <h4>Asia â€” 9 countries</h4>
  <p>
    South Korea, Japan, Singapore, Brunei, United Arab Emirates, Qatar,
    Indonesia, Kyrgyzstan, and Vietnam.
  </p>

  <p>
    The list most recently expanded on August 20, 2026, when Kyrgyzstan and
    Vietnam were added, bringing the total from 55 to 57 countries.
  </p>

  <h3>Your nationality matters, not where you live or where your trip begins</h3>
  <p>
    Eligibility for this part of the policy is based on your
    nationality/travel document.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, a U.S. citizen living in the United Kingdom does not need to
    begin the trip in the United States. The route itself is evaluated
    separately under the Country/Region A &rarr; Mainland China &rarr;
    Country/Region B rule explained above.
  </p>

  <h3>Is there an age restriction?</h3>
  <p>
    No age restriction is stated under the current policy. Children may use the
    240-hour visa-free transit policy if they meet the same applicable
    requirements, including nationality, travel-document, itinerary and entry
    conditions.
  </p>

  <p>
    For passport validity and U.S.-specific passport guidance, see the Document
    Checklist in Section 8.
  </p>

  <h3>What if your nationality is not on the list?</h3>
  <p>
    You cannot rely on the 240-hour policy solely on the basis of transit if
    your nationality is not one of the 57 currently covered countries.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, another Chinese visa-free policy may apply depending on your
    circumstances, such as:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>the 24-hour visa-free transit policy;</li>
    <li>a unilateral or mutual visa exemption;</li>
    <li>or the separate Hainan visa-free entry policy.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>We compare these policies later in this guide.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How the 240 Hours Are Calculated</h2>

  <p>
    The 240-hour period does not normally begin at the moment your flight lands.
  </p>

  <p>
    Under current National Immigration Administration guidance, the visa-free
    stay period is calculated from 00:00 on the day following the day of entry.
  </p>

  <h3>Example</h3>

  <p>
    Suppose you enter Mainland China on June 1 at 3:00 PM.
  </p>

  <p>
    Your 240-hour clock begins at June 2 at 00:00.
  </p>

  <p>
    240 hours equals 10 full 24-hour days, so the 240-hour period runs until
    June 12 at 00:00.
  </p>

  <p>
    In practical terms, the ten counted days are June 2 through June 11, and
    you would need to leave within the period authorized by immigration.
  </p>

  <h3>Why can your actual time in China be longer than exactly 240 elapsed hours?</h3>

  <p>
    Because the official 240-hour calculation begins at 00:00 on the day after
    entry, the time between your admission on the arrival day and midnight is
    not part of the 240-hour count.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, if you enter at 9:00 AM, there may be almost 15 hours before
    the 240-hour clock begins.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, you should not plan your departure solely from your own
    calculation. The immigration authority will complete the temporary-entry
    formalities and indicate your authorized stay. Follow the period authorized
    by immigration, particularly if it differs from what you expected.
  </p>

  <h3>Does the departure flight have to leave within the 240-hour period?</h3>

  <p>
    Yes. Your confirmed onward travel must be scheduled within the permitted
    transit period. Current NIA requirements call for onward travel to a third
    country or region with a confirmed date and arrangement within 240 hours,
    or other qualifying proof.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Important:</strong> Flight delays, cancellations and other
    emergencies can create an overstay risk. We explain what to do if you
    cannot leave before your authorized stay expires later in this guide.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Eligible Ports and Permitted Stay Areas</h2>

  <p>
    The 240-hour policy does not apply at every possible border crossing or
    allow unrestricted travel throughout Mainland China.
  </p>

  <p>
    There are currently 65 designated entry and exit ports across 24
    provincial-level regions.
  </p>

  <h3>The 24 participating regions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Beijing</li>
    <li>Tianjin</li>
    <li>Hebei</li>
    <li>Liaoning</li>
    <li>Heilongjiang</li>
    <li>Shanghai</li>
    <li>Jiangsu</li>
    <li>Zhejiang</li>
    <li>Fujian</li>
    <li>Shandong</li>
    <li>Henan</li>
    <li>Hubei</li>
    <li>Hunan</li>
    <li>Guangdong</li>
    <li>Guangxi</li>
    <li>Chongqing</li>
    <li>Sichuan</li>
    <li>Yunnan</li>
    <li>Shaanxi</li>
    <li>Shanxi</li>
    <li>Anhui</li>
    <li>Jiangxi</li>
    <li>Hainan</li>
    <li>Guizhou</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Travelers may make cross-provincial trips within the designated permitted
    areas rather than being restricted to the city where they entered.
  </p>

  <h3>Important: "24 regions" does not mean every place within all 24 regions is automatically permitted</h3>

  <p>
    The permitted geography differs by region. Some participating provinces
    permit travel throughout the entire province, while others cover only
    particular cities or administrative areas.
  </p>

  <p>For example, NIA guidance states that after the 240-hour expansion:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Anhui, Hainan and Guizhou allow travel throughout the province;</li>
    <li>Fujian, Hubei and Shaanxi were expanded to the whole province;</li>
    <li>Shanxi's permitted area includes Taiyuan and Datong;</li>
    <li>Jiangxi's permitted area includes Nanchang and Jingdezhen;</li>
    <li>Guangxi covers a specified group of cities rather than automatically every location in the autonomous region.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Therefore, do not simply ask "Is this province covered?" Instead ask:
    "Is the specific city or area I plan to visit within the currently permitted area?"
  </p>

  <h3>Your entry and exit ports must also be eligible</h3>

  <p>
    The 65 participating ports include different types of border crossings, not
    only airports.
  </p>

  <p>Depending on the location, eligible ports include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>international airports;</li>
    <li>passenger seaports;</li>
    <li>certain railway ports;</li>
    <li>and certain land-border crossings.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For example, the policy now includes West Kowloon Station of the
    Guangzhou-Shenzhen-Hong Kong Express Rail Link, following the addition of
    five Guangdong ports in November 2025.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is important because older online guides may still say that travelers
    cannot enter Mainland China from Hong Kong by high-speed rail under the policy.
  </p>

  <h3>Can you enter through one eligible port and leave through another?</h3>

  <p>
    Yes, provided your itinerary continues to satisfy the policy requirements
    and both ports are applicable to the 240-hour program.
  </p>

  <p>
    Official guidance states that qualifying travelers may travel within
    designated areas across participating regions and depart through an
    applicable port under the policy.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, a traveler may not necessarily have to use Shanghai Pudong
    Airport for both entry and exit. A route involving a different eligible
    departure port may be possible, provided all other route, geographic and
    timing requirements are met.
  </p>

  <h3>Check the eligible ports and permitted stay areas</h3>

  <p>
    Because the permitted area depends on where you enter and travel, you should
    check your planned entry port, exit port, and every Mainland China
    destination before relying on the 240-hour policy.
  </p>

  <p>
    China's National Immigration Administration publishes the official list of
    the 65 eligible ports and their permitted stay areas.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="https://en.nia.gov.cn/n147413/c187308/content.html">
      See the official NIA list of eligible ports and permitted stay areas
    </a>
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Document Checklist</h2>

  <p>
    It is helpful to separate published eligibility requirements from documents
    that are simply useful to carry.
  </p>

  <h3>Documents required under the published 240-hour policy</h3>

  <p>
    Current National Immigration Administration guidance requires the following
    core items.
  </p>

  <h4>1. A valid international travel document</h4>

  <p>
    Your travel document must generally have at least three months of remaining
    validity for purposes of the 240-hour policy.
  </p>

  <p>
    As noted above, travelers should also check any stricter passport
    requirements that apply to their nationality or onward destination.
  </p>

  <h4>2. Proof that you can enter your next country or region</h4>

  <p>
    You must satisfy the entry requirements of the country or region you will
    travel to after Mainland China.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, if your onward destination requires you to have a visa, an
    electronic travel authorization, or another entry document, you should have
    that authorization before relying on the China transit policy.
  </p>

  <h4>3. Confirmed onward travel to a third country or region</h4>

  <p>
    You need confirmed onward travel within the permitted period.
  </p>

  <p>
    NIA guidance refers to an onward ticket with a confirmed date and
    arrangement to a third country or region within 240 hours, or other
    qualifying proof documents.
  </p>

  <p>
    Your onward itinerary therefore needs to demonstrate both when you will
    leave Mainland China and where you are going next.
  </p>

  <h4>4. Arrival Card information</h4>

  <p>
    Travelers must provide the required arrival information.
  </p>

  <p>
    Since November 20, 2025, foreigners can complete China's Arrival Card
    information online before traveling through official NIA channels.
  </p>

  <p>
    Travelers who do not complete it in advance can generally complete the
    information after arriving through an online system, smart devices or a
    paper Arrival Card at the port.
  </p>

  <h4>5. Immigration inspection</h4>

  <p>
    You must cooperate with the immigration inspection authority at your port
    of entry.
  </p>

  <p>
    Meeting the document requirements does not automatically grant admission.
    Immigration authorities review the application and complete the
    temporary-entry formalities after arrival.
  </p>

  <h3>Documents we recommend carrying even when they are not listed as core national eligibility requirements</h3>

  <p>
    The following are useful because airline staff or immigration officers may
    want to understand your trip quickly:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>accommodation reservations or the address where you will stay;</li>
    <li>a simple copy of your China itinerary;</li>
    <li>offline or printed copies of inbound and onward tickets;</li>
    <li>evidence of onward-destination entry authorization where applicable;</li>
    <li>contact information for your hotel or host;</li>
    <li>a copy or saved link to the current official 240-hour policy.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Practical recommendation — not presented as a universal legal requirement:
    keeping the key documents available offline can help if you do not yet have
    mobile data after landing.
  </p>

  <h3>Do you need a hotel reservation to qualify?</h3>

  <p>
    A hotel reservation is not listed among the core national eligibility
    conditions in the NIA requirements we reviewed. The published conditions
    focus on nationality, travel-document validity, onward-destination
    eligibility, confirmed onward travel, Arrival Card information and
    immigration inspection.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, you may need accommodation details for arrival formalities, and
    accommodation-registration rules apply after entry. We cover those
    requirements later in this guide.
  </p>

  <h3>U.S. passport holders: use the stricter passport guidance</h3>

  <p>For U.S. citizens, Travel.State.gov currently states that the passport should:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>be valid for at least six months beyond the date of arrival;</li>
    <li>have at least two blank pages;</li>
    <li>and not be the 12-page U.S. emergency passport when seeking visa-free entry.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    This is stricter than the three-month validity condition stated in NIA's
    240-hour eligibility rules. For a U.S.-focused traveler guide, we recommend
    planning around the six-month requirement rather than relying on the lower
    three-month policy threshold.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Airline Check-In and Chinese Immigration</h2>

  <p>
    Using the 240-hour visa-free transit policy involves two separate stages:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>getting accepted for your journey by your airline or other international carrier; and</li>
    <li>receiving approval for temporary entry from Chinese immigration after you arrive.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>These are not the same decision.</p>

  <h3>Before boarding your flight to China</h3>

  <p>
    Keep the core documents from Section 8 easy to access, especially your
    passport, confirmed onward travel and any required authorization for your
    next destination. Airline procedures vary, so be prepared to explain that
    you plan to use China's 240-hour visa-free transit policy and show your
    onward itinerary. Official Beijing guidance advises travelers to inform the
    airline when boarding.
  </p>

  <p>
    Practical tip: Keep your onward ticket and key documents available offline
    or in print.
  </p>

  <h3>Do you need to apply at a Chinese embassy before traveling?</h3>

  <p>
    No separate embassy application is required for the 240-hour visa-free
    transit permit itself.
  </p>

  <p>
    Current official Shanghai guidance states that eligible travelers can apply
    upon arrival at the Chinese port of entry and do not need to contact a
    Chinese embassy in advance. Final approval remains subject to on-site
    immigration review.
  </p>

  <p>
    You may complete your Arrival Card information in advance, as explained in
    Section 8, but this is different from obtaining advance approval for
    240-hour visa-free transit.
  </p>

  <h3>What happens after you arrive in China?</h3>

  <p>
    The exact layout and procedure can vary by port, but the general process is:
  </p>

  <h4>1. Follow signs for 24/240-hour visa-free transit or temporary entry permits</h4>

  <p>
    Major participating ports may have designated counters or special channels
    for travelers using the policy. Shanghai, for example, provides special
    channels for 240-hour visa-free transit at its participating ports.
  </p>

  <h4>2. Present your documents</h4>

  <p>
    Present the core documents listed in Section 8 and any additional
    information requested by the immigration officer.
  </p>

  <h4>3. Undergo immigration inspection</h4>

  <p>
    The immigration authority will review your itinerary and other requirements.
  </p>

  <p>
    If your application is approved, you will receive authorization for
    temporary entry under the 240-hour visa-free transit policy. Official
    Shanghai guidance states that a temporary entry permit sticker may be placed
    in the passport and can then be used for purposes such as hotel check-in and
    accommodation registration.
  </p>

  <h4>4. Continue through the normal arrival process</h4>

  <p>
    After immigration approval, travelers can proceed with baggage collection,
    customs procedures and entry into China according to the arrangements at
    that port. Official Beijing guidance describes the sequence as
    temporary-entry processing followed by baggage collection and customs.
  </p>

  <h3>Does meeting all the published requirements guarantee entry?</h3>

  <p>No.</p>

  <p>
    Even when your nationality, route, ports, timing and documents appear to
    satisfy the published rules, the final decision is made through on-site
    immigration inspection.
  </p>

  <p>
    For that reason, throughout this guide we use language such as "Your
    itinerary appears to meet the published requirements" rather than "Your
    entry is guaranteed."
  </p>
</section>
